A lot more. Once you start looking for them, these aren’t isolated tricks so much as recurring game geometries—ways that choices, evidence, timing, information, and consequences get arranged so the surface game differs from the actual one.

* Win-win / lose-lose / win-lose / lose-win — the basic payoff quadrants. Useful because many situations advertised as competitive are actually coordination problems, and vice versa.
* I win now, you pay later — benefits are immediate and concentrated; costs are delayed or dispersed.
* You choose, I define what your choice means — apparent agency with semantic control retained elsewhere.
* You choose the action, I choose the metric — whatever happens can be scored favorably afterward.
* I choose the test after seeing the result — retrospective criteria manufacture success.
* Heads counts, tails disappears — favorable observations enter the dataset; unfavorable ones vanish.
* Heads is evidence, tails is “noise” — asymmetric treatment of signal and error.
* My mistake is an exception; yours is a pattern — attribution asymmetry.
* My motive is context; yours is character — situational explanations for self, dispositional explanations for others.
* Same behavior, different label — “ours” becomes courage, “theirs” recklessness; “ours” strategy, “theirs” manipulation.
* Same rule, unequal enforcement — symmetry in stated law, asymmetry in application.
* Equal procedure, unequal starting position — superficially fair rules preserve an underlying structural advantage.
* Equal outcome, unequal cost — two actors arrive at the same endpoint after radically different effort or sacrifice.
* Equal input, unequal leverage — one actor sits at a network bottleneck, so the same action has more effect.
* Small cause, large cascade — leverage point / tipping-point dynamics.
* Large cause, tiny effect — pushing against a highly damped system.
* One-way door — a decision is easy to enter and expensive or impossible to reverse.
* Two-way door — reversible decisions should often be made faster because experimentation is cheap.
* Ratchet — a system can move in both directions locally, but accumulated change persists mainly in one direction.
* Hysteresis — the path into a state differs from the path out; reversing the input doesn’t simply reverse the outcome.
* Path dependence — what is possible now depends on the sequence by which you arrived.
* First-mover lock-in — an early arbitrary choice becomes expensive to replace after infrastructure accumulates around it.
* Last-mover advantage — waiting reveals everyone else’s information before committing.
* Option value — delaying commitment preserves future branches.
* Burn-the-boats commitment — deliberately destroying options changes everyone’s incentives, including your own.
* Credible threat through self-binding — making yourself unable to retreat can change the opponent’s calculation.
* Chicken — whoever can convincingly remove their steering wheel may force the other side to turn.
* Prisoner’s dilemma — individually rational moves produce a collectively worse equilibrium.
* Stag hunt — the best outcome requires trust; the safer outcome requires little coordination.
* Volunteer’s dilemma — everyone benefits if someone bears the cost, so everyone waits for someone else.
* Tragedy of the commons — locally rational extraction destroys the shared resource.
* Free rider — consume collective benefit without paying corresponding cost.
* Public-goods multiplier — small private sacrifices compound into large group value when enough participants contribute.
* Principal-agent problem — the person acting has different incentives from the person bearing the consequences.
* Goodhart’s law — once a measurement becomes the target, actors optimize the measurement rather than the underlying objective.
* Campbell’s law — stronger stakes attached to a metric create stronger pressure to corrupt the metric.
* Cobra effect — reward solving a problem and people may manufacture more of the problem to earn the reward.
* Perverse incentive — the reward function teaches the opposite behavior from the stated goal.
* Moral hazard — insulation from downside encourages greater risk-taking.
* Adverse selection — the rules attract exactly the population you least wanted.
* Survivorship bias — study what survived and infer rules while missing the failures that disappeared.
* Base-rate neglect — vivid individual evidence overwhelms the larger statistical prior.
* Availability cascade — repetition makes something easier to recall, which gets mistaken for prevalence or truth.
* Preference cascade — people publicly imitate a position they privately doubt until apparent consensus becomes self-sustaining.
* Pluralistic ignorance — most people privately disagree while each assumes everyone else agrees.
* Common-knowledge threshold — it isn’t enough that everyone knows; sometimes everyone must know that everyone knows.
* Coordination beacon — an arbitrary symbol becomes powerful simply because everyone expects everyone else to use it.
* Schelling point — people converge without communication because one option is unusually salient.
* Beauty contest — you aren’t predicting reality; you’re predicting what everyone else predicts everyone else will predict.
* Keynesian second-order game — success depends less on your judgment of value than your judgment of others’ judgments.
* Reflexivity — beliefs about reality change behavior, which changes the reality being believed about.
* Self-fulfilling prophecy — expectation helps create its own evidence.
* Self-defeating prophecy — prediction induces preventative behavior that makes the prediction fail.
* Observer effect in social systems — measuring or announcing a behavior changes the behavior itself.
* Streisand effect — attempts to suppress information increase attention to it.
* Reactance — restricting choice can increase desire for the prohibited option.
* Forbidden-fruit premium — scarcity or prohibition adds symbolic value independent of utility.
* Decoy effect — introduce an intentionally inferior third option to make one of the original choices look better.
* Anchoring — the first number/frame changes subsequent judgments even when arbitrary.
* Contrast effect — an object’s perceived value changes because of what it is compared against.
* Framing effect — equivalent outcomes generate different choices depending on whether they are represented as gains or losses.
* Default effect — the preselected option gains power because inaction chooses it automatically.
* Choice architecture — control behavior by arranging pathways rather than issuing commands.
* Dark pattern — make the desired action frictionless and the undesired action tedious.
* Friction asymmetry — one direction is technically permitted but made operationally expensive.
* Salience steering — don’t forbid alternatives; simply make one option much easier to notice.
* Agenda setting — control not what people think, but what they spend time thinking about.
* Question substitution — replace a hard question with an easier adjacent one without noticing.
* Frame capture — debating inside someone else’s categories grants their assumptions before the argument begins.
* Premise smuggling — hide a contested proposition inside an apparently innocent question.
* Overton movement — shift the boundaries of what can be discussed before trying to win the argument itself.
* Reference-class manipulation — change the comparison group and the same object becomes exceptional or mediocre.
* Denominator games — the numerator stays fixed while the chosen denominator changes the apparent rate.
* Scale games — zooming temporal or geographic resolution makes the same trend appear reversed.
* Aggregation reversal / Simpson’s paradox — a trend seen in every subgroup can reverse when groups are combined.
* Local optimum trap — every immediate improvement prevents reaching a better distant state.
* Exploration-exploitation trap — overusing what currently works prevents discovery of something better.
* Escalation of commitment — prior investment becomes a reason to invest more despite deteriorating prospects.
* Sunk-cost loop — past cost improperly influences future decisions.
* Success trap — yesterday’s winning strategy suppresses adaptations needed tomorrow.
* Competency trap — becoming excellent at one method makes alternatives look worse because you haven’t practiced them.
* Red Queen dynamic — you must keep improving merely to maintain relative position.
* Arms race — each defensive adaptation creates incentives for a stronger offense, recursively.
* Security dilemma — one side’s defensive preparation appears offensive to another, causing both to escalate.
* Mutual hostage — both sides become safer because each can severely punish betrayal.
* Assured interdependence — instead of mutual destruction, structure things so both profit more from continuity than rupture.
* Weak-link system — performance is governed by the worst component.
* Strong-link system — one exceptional component can dominate system performance.
* Bottleneck migration — solve one constraint and another becomes the limiting factor.
* Constraint inversion — remove what looked like the obstacle and discover it was actually stabilizing the system.
* Second-order effect — solving the visible problem changes incentives and creates a new problem.
* Third-order effect — actors adapt to the solution to the second-order effect.
* Hydra effect — suppress one node and several substitutes emerge.
* Whack-a-mole — local interventions displace rather than eliminate the underlying process.
* Balloon effect — squeeze a system in one location and pressure reappears elsewhere.
* Rebound effect — making something more efficient can increase total consumption rather than reduce it.
* Jevons paradox — sufficiently large efficiency gains can increase resource use by lowering effective cost.
* Antifragility — certain systems don’t merely withstand variation; bounded stress improves them.
* Hormesis — a small dose of stress creates adaptation while a large dose causes damage.
* Goldilocks zone — too little and too much fail for different reasons; function exists inside a band.
* Edge of chaos — maximum adaptability can occur between rigid order and total randomness.
* Ugly third — when a binary appears exhaustive, search for a dimension on which both poles are merely variants of the same thing.

And there’s a higher-order pattern connecting almost all of these:

\boxed{\text{The visible move is often not where the leverage lives.}}

Sometimes the important control variable is the payoff function. Sometimes it is the timing. Sometimes it is the denominator, default, frame, available branches, reversibility, information distribution, or who gets to classify the outcome afterward.

So when we inspect a game, argument, market, organization, story, or algorithm, the Pantheon-style interrogation could become:

\text{What are the apparent choices?}

\text{Who constructed the choice space?}

\text{Where are the asymmetric costs and optionality?}

\text{What variable is being held invisible?}

\text{And what happens if we rotate the board rather than make another move?}

That last one is where many of the really interesting patterns live.